//! A terminal session: the PTY, the reader thread and the grid.
//!
//! The crate's encapsulation contract lives in `lib.rs`; here are its two
//! practical consequences: alacritty's `EventListener` is turned into our
//! `Wake` in `Adapter`, and the `Term` lock is taken **only** at these call
//! sites: `frame`, `resize`, the selection path (`set_selection`,
//! `extend_selection`, `select_all`, `update_selection`, `clear_selection`,
//! `selection_text`), the scroll path (`scroll_wheel`,
//! `scroll_page`), sending user input (`send_input`: clearing the selection,
//! returning to the bottom and the mode query for the arrow are in the same
//! lock), `paste`'s mode query (`bracketed_paste`), the live change of the
//! terminal options (`set_terminal_options`) and clearing the screen
//! (`clear_to_start`, `clear_scrollback`: returning to the bottom, scrolling,
//! scrollback, selection and the `2J` generation in the same lock).
//! The lock **order** is the same everywhere — `term` first, `size` second;
//! when adding a new site this order is followed, because if the two locks
//! are taken in reverse order a deadlock arises. `theme`, `shell` and `search`
//! are **leaf** locks outside this order: while one is held no other lock is
//! taken, so which lock it is taken under does not matter — `frame` takes
//! the theme's copy before `term` and releases it, the colour query reads
//! while `term` is held, `set_theme` writes on its own; `frame` borrows the
//! search pattern before `term` and puts it back after releasing
//! (`SearchSlot`).
//!
//! The rule for `shell` is one-directional and the direction is this:
//! **`term` is not taken while `shell` is held.** The reader thread already
//! writes `shell` *under* `term` — the reader loop holds the terminal lease
//! for the whole of `pty_read` (`reader.rs`, `_terminal_lease`; a copy of
//! alacritty's loop that preserves this contract exactly) and our
//! `TappedPty::read` runs under that guard — so the `term` → `shell` order
//! is the reader's own order and cannot deadlock. The other edge of the only
//! cycle that could close would be the reverse direction, so `frame`'s second
//! phase (the block stripes) takes `shell` **after `term` is released**, and
//! `shell_state` and `set_terminal_options` do the same. (An older rationale
//! here put the prohibition in the reverse direction and made the reader's
//! own order look dangerous.)

